  "text": "Here's the completed function `remove_adjacent_duplicates` that processes a list of integers and removes consecutive duplicates, returning a new list with only one instance of each consecutive group of duplicates.\n\n```python\nimport unittest\n\ndef remove_adjacent_duplicates(lst):\n    if not lst:\n        return []\n    \n    result = [lst[0]]  # Start with the first element\n    for num in lst[1:]:\n        if num != result[-1]:\n            result.append(num)\n    return result\n\nclass TestRemoveAdjacentDuplicates(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_basic_functionality(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4]), [1, 2, 3, 4])\n    \n    def test_repeated_elements(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([5, 5, 5, 6, 7, 7]), [5, 6, 7])\n    \n    def test_multiple_duplicates(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([1, 2, 3, 4, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5]), [1, 2, 3, 4, 5])\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    unittest.main()\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n- The function starts with the first element of the list.\n- It then iterates through the rest of the list and appends an element to the result list only if it's different from the last element in the result list.\n- This effectively removes consecutive duplicates.\n- The function returns a new list rather than modifying the original.\n\n### Example:\n```python\nremove_adjacent_duplicates([1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4])  # returns [1, 2, 3, 4]\n```",
